From fd3611ee7a15fc3f1403902515ae59d34ea62642 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:45:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 45/49] Red team fix 4: the listings that validate lock-wait
 reads are taken where stock lists

Once the lock was confirmed, a goroutine listed the snapshot files and then
the index files at once and decided from that what to keep of the reads of
the wait. For restore that index listing came before the selected snapshot
had been read, where stock lists the index files after it. On a backend
whose listings lag behind, the index file of the newest snapshot could
become visible in between: stock sees it and restores, this build had
already decided on the older listing and failed on a healthy repository.

The rule now holds for every command with wait work (backup, restore, ls,
forget, prune): a listing that decides about reads of the wait is taken no
earlier than the point at which stock lists that directory. The snapshot
files are listed when the command asks for the listing, which is where it
lists them in stock. The index files are listed when the command asks for
the index, which is where stock calls LoadIndex: for restore, backup and
forget after the snapshots were read. Nothing is listed at confirmation any
more. With any backend this build knows at each point what stock knows
there; what was read during the wait only saves the downloads.

Tests (seat 1 R2, carried in): TestRestoreListsIndexAfterSnapshotRead (the
restore command, production 200 ms delay) and
TestLockWaitIndexListedAfterSnapshotRead fail before this commit and pass
after. The reviewer's backend held the snapshot read until the early index
listing had happened and failed after five seconds without one; here it
goes on after 300 ms, since the listing no longer comes first.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
